      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1696157260,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "So this is for the case where we can detect connection migration in the one direction, but the other direction has a zero length DCID so we couldn't detect it without attaching it to the 5-tuple? (A case [warned](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000.html#section-5.1-8) to be fraught with peril.)\n\nThat does seem to violate [RFC 9000](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000.html#section-9.5-3):\n\n> An endpoint MUST NOT reuse a connection ID when sending from more than one local address -- for example, when initiating connection migration as described in Section 9.2 or when probing a new network path as described in Section 9.1\n\nThe client should be using the new connection ID provided in the NEW_CONNECTION_ID QUIC frame in frame 13 instead of re-using the existing one from its new address. Ideally we'd have an expert info for that.\n\nThis does seem to work in this case, though I think we only need to do this for short packets, perhaps?",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
          "created_at": "2023-12-15T10:56:01.027Z",
